GroovyMAME nogpu: line pacing, each datagram sent at its raster time (-mister_linepacing 1)

-mister_pacing spreads a frame with a constant BYTE rate (one raster line of raw pixels
per line time). That matches the raster only while every line has the same size. With
-mister_compression qoi a flat line is a few bytes and a detailed one a full raw line, so
a beam-racing receiver (Groovy_TaNGer: a 32-line ring) gets the flat parts of the picture
far too early: on Sonic 2 in RGB565 the coded frame arrived ~200 lines ahead, the ring
kept only its last 32 lines, and on flat skies (one byte = up to 62 pixels) the bytes
came faster than any decoder at 50 MHz, so its input FIFO overflowed (seen on HW).

With -mister_linepacing 1 every datagram gets its own departure time instead: t0 (the
CMD_BLIT) + the raster time of the first line it carries. The fq qdisc holds it until
then (SO_TXTIME, CLOCK_MONOTONIC, no privilege needed); sendmsg returns at once, so the
emulation thread never waits, exactly as with -mister_pacing.

- a datagram carries at most 4 lines (MAX_LINES_PER_DGRAM), so no line of it arrives
  more than 4 lines early (a 1472-byte datagram could otherwise hold ~50 flat lines)
- works for raw and qoi (both have line boundaries); lz4 has none and is sent as before
- every other datagram on the socket (CMD_AUDIO, next CMD_BLIT, ...) is stamped too, no
  earlier than the last one queued: fq keeps a flow sorted by send time (Linux >= 5.7),
  and an unstamped datagram would overtake the waiting pixels and land in the middle
  of the blit payload
- replaces -mister_pacing (ignored, with a message, when both are given)
- not Linux, or SO_TXTIME refused: "line pacing not supported", sent as before

Linux only. The interface must use the fq qdisc, like -mister_pacing: other qdiscs
(fq_codel, the default) accept SO_TXTIME and silently ignore it. A raw 320x240 frame is
~105 datagrams queued at once, more than fq's default flow_limit of 100:
  tc qdisc replace dev <if> root fq flow_limit 1000

Depends on: groovymame-mister-rgb565.patch, groovymame-mister-pacing.patch and
groovymame-mister-qoi.patch (it uses their m_bpp, m_pacing and m_fb_qoi).
Base: substring/GroovyMAME 4ce01b9 (libretro-driver) + rgb565 + pacing + qoi.
Apply with
  git apply -p1 groovymame-mister-linepacing.patch
Checked: applies on that base; drawnogpu.cpp and osdobj_common.cpp pass a syntax-only
compile with the libretro driver's release flags (g++ -std=c++20 -Wall -Werror). NOT
run yet.

